# Code Style & Quality

## Standard & flags

- **C11** (`-std=c11`)
- `-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror`
- `-D_XOPEN_SOURCE=700` (POSIX.1-2008 + XSI: `st_blocks`, `S_ISVTX`, `lstat`, `readlink`)
- `-DLS_TOTO_WIN32_FS` when the Makefile POSIX header probe fails (Windows/Git Bash)
- Zero warnings — all treated as errors

## Formatting

- **Indentation:** 4 spaces. No tabs.
- **Function definitions:** opening brace on its own line.
- **Line length:** prefer ≤80 characters where practical.
- **Files end with a single newline.**

## Module conventions

- **`src/main.c`** stays thin — call `ls_toto_parse_args()`, dispatch to
  help/version, call `ls_toto_run()`, and exit. No filesystem, sorting, or
  formatting logic.
- **`include/ls_toto.h`** — version string, exit code enum, and
  `ls_toto_run()` declaration.
- **`include/ls_toto_cli.h`** — CLI flag string macros (`LS_TOTO_ARG_*`),
  `struct ls_toto_opts`, parser declarations.
- **One concern per `src/ls_toto_*.c`** (`cli`, `emit`, `fs`, `sort`, `format`,
  `out`, `list`) — do not collapse back into a monolith without reason.
- **Platform code lives only in `ls_toto_fs.c`** (plus `isatty` handling in
  `ls_toto_list.c` / `ls_toto_out.c` if needed). Every other module works on the
  platform-neutral `struct ls_toto_entry`.
- **Platform guard:** `#ifdef LS_TOTO_WIN32_FS` in source and test files; do
  not use bare `#if defined(_WIN32)` alone (Cygwin/MSYS with the POSIX headers
  should keep the POSIX path).
- **Pure modules** (`ls_toto_cli.c`, `ls_toto_sort.c`, `ls_toto_format.c`)
  must not perform I/O or call platform APIs, so they can be linked into tests.

## Naming

| Kind | Style | Examples |
|------|-------|----------|
| Functions | `snake_case` | `ls_toto_run`, `ls_toto_parse_args`, `argv_has_exact`, `print_help` |
| Types | `snake_case` structs, no typedef-hiding of pointers | `struct ls_toto_opts`, `struct ls_toto_entry` |
| Macros / constants | `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` | `LS_TOTO_VERSION_STRING`, `LS_TOTO_ARG_ALL`, `LS_TOTO_OUT_BUFSZ` |
| Headers | `LS_TOTO_*_H` guards | `LS_TOTO_H`, `LS_TOTO_CLI_H`, `LS_TOTO_FS_H` |

Public API prefix: **`ls_toto_`** for module entry points.

## Memory

- Heap allocation is **allowed** (directory listings are unbounded), unlike
  whoami-toto.
- Entry arrays grow by **doubling** (`realloc`); start from a small fixed
  capacity.
- Copy names with **`ls_toto_strdup()`** (`malloc` + `memcpy`) — `strdup` is
  not ISO C11.
- **Every** `malloc` / `realloc` result is checked; on failure call
  `ls_toto_emit_error("malloc")` and exit with `LS_TOTO_EXIT_SERIOUS`.
- Free every entry array and name before returning from a directory listing;
  recursion (`-R`) must not keep parent arrays alive longer than needed.
- `getpwuid()` / `getgrgid()` return **static internal buffers** — do not
  `free()` them; copy the name into the entry's fixed-size owner/group field
  before the next lookup.
- `make debug` (ASan + UBSan) must run clean: no leaks, no overflows.

## Output rules (`ls_toto_out.c`)

- All listing output goes through the buffered writer: a static
  `char buf[LS_TOTO_OUT_BUFSZ]` (8 KiB), appended with `ls_toto_out_*()` and
  flushed with `try_write_all(STDOUT_FILENO, buf, len)`.
- Flush when the buffer would overflow and once before exit.
- No `printf` / `fprintf` / `puts` / `fputs` / `fwrite` for listing output.
  `snprintf` into a local buffer is fine for numeric fields.
- `--help` / `--version` text may use stdio.
- `exit()` on unrecoverable error (write / allocation failure) is acceptable.

## Errors

- **stderr only:** `ls-toto: <context>: <strerror(errno)>` for system errors.
- **stderr only:** `ls-toto: cannot access '<path>': <strerror(errno)>`.
- **stderr only:** `ls-toto: cannot open directory '<path>': <strerror(errno)>`.
- **stderr only:** `ls-toto: invalid option -- '<c>'` or
  `ls-toto: unrecognized option '<arg>'`, each followed by the hint line.
- Save `errno` immediately after the failing call, before any other libc call.
- Implemented in `ls_toto_emit.c`.

## Comments

- Chain-of-thought block at top of each `src/*.c` and test file.
- Contract comments on non-trivial functions: preconditions, postconditions,
  return value, error behaviour, ownership of returned memory.
- Comment non-obvious decisions (platform guards, static-buffer lifetime of
  `getpwuid`/`getgrgid`, six-month time cutoff, Makefile header probe); not
  obvious code.

## Tests

- Plain `assert()` — no external test framework.
- One scenario per `tests/test_<module>_<name>.c`; runner in `tests/test_runner.c`.
- Print `PASS: <name>` on success; `SKIP: <name>` for POSIX-only cases when
  `LS_TOTO_WIN32_FS` is defined.

## What not to do

- Do not compile as C++.
- Do not link extra libraries beyond **libc** (POSIX) or **libc + kernel32**
  (Win32 filesystem path).
- Do not use `gets`, unbounded `scanf`, `strcpy` / `strcat` / `sprintf` into
  fixed buffers, or ignore `write()` byte counts.
- Do not use `strdup`, `scandir`, or `getopt_long` (not ISO C / GNU
  extensions) — option parsing is hand-rolled.
- Do not call `setlocale()` or use locale-aware collation (`strcoll`).
- Do not use stdio for listing output — project standard is the buffered
  raw `write()` writer.
- Do not call `getpwuid_r` / `getgrgid_r` unless thread-safety is explicitly
  required.
- Do not add options beyond the supported set in `project-overview.mdc`.
